------p2mBoundary7f3a9c2b
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="explanation"

Partition R into the low-weight indices w(i) <= c and the high-weight indices w(i) > c. Their cardinalities add to #R, so the assumed bound for the low part gives #(high) >= (1-eta)#R. Multiplication by the nonnegative cutoff c preserves this inequality. Every high index contributes at least c, and all remaining weights are nonnegative, hence c#(high) <= sum_{i in R} w(i). Combining these inequalities gives the claimed lower bound. The original same-file theorem cutoff_mul_high_count_le_sum is retained with its complete proof as an inline helper.
